# src/kernel.manifest — the thatteOS kernel, in one translation unit
#
# Author: Manish Jagdish Thatte
# © Manish Jagdish Thatte
#
# WHY A MANIFEST AND NOT `use`. ManiT has no cross-file bodies: `use m;`
# registers m's SIGNATURES and drops its bodies, so the program type-checks
# and then fails at link with `Undefined label: m::f` (T3) or
# `use of undefined value '@m_f'` (LLVM). Re-measured against the compiler on
# 30 August 2026, not read out of a document — it is still true.
#
# Until it stops being true, a program that spans files is a program whose
# modules are CONCATENATED, and `studioMani/build.sh` has done exactly this
# for thirteen modules since before this file existed. The manifest is that
# list, written down where a reader can find it rather than buried in a shell
# script, and `tools/merge_modules.py` is what turns it into the merged source
# plus a LINE MAP so a diagnostic in the merged file can be traced back to the
# module and line it came from.
#
# WHAT THIS COSTS: ONE FLAT NAMESPACE. Every module's top-level names share it,
# so two modules cannot both define `priv_name`. That is not a defect of the
# mechanism — it is the mechanism telling the truth about a kernel that had
# `priv_name` six times, `PCB` three times and `state_name` three times, each
# copy free to drift from the others because nothing ever compiled them
# together. Deduplicating them was the work of ENHANCEMENT_PLAN.md §1.2.
#
# ORDER. A callee may appear anywhere; a STRUCT must precede its first use.
# The order below is therefore dependency order, roughly bottom-up: ternary
# primitives, then kernel services, then memory, then filesystem, then IPC,
# then drivers and syscalls, then security, and `boot.mt` last because
# `kernel_main` calls everything above it.
#
# ADDING A MODULE: put it here, in the position its struct dependencies need,
# and run `bash build.sh`. There is no second place to register it — and that
# is deliberate, because `userspace/build.sh`'s PROGRAMS list omitted three
# programs for weeks and nothing noticed.

# --- ternary primitives -----------------------------------------------------
src/kernel/tmin2.mt

# --- kernel services --------------------------------------------------------
src/kernel/klog.mt
src/kernel/privilege.mt
src/security/capability.mt
src/kernel/panic.mt
src/kernel/guard.mt
src/kernel/context.mt
src/kernel/process.mt
src/kernel/scheduler.mt
src/kernel/timer.mt
src/kernel/signal.mt
src/kernel/interrupt.mt

# --- memory management ------------------------------------------------------
src/mm/pgtable.mt
src/mm/vmem.mt
src/mm/mmu.mt

# --- filesystem -------------------------------------------------------------
# inode.mt defines Inode and TritFS; fdtable.mt defines FileDesc and FdTable;
# perms.mt is the permission layer; tritfs.mt composes them. Before the merge
# tritfs.mt carried its OWN copy of all 28 of those declarations and had not
# one of its own — see ENHANCEMENT_PLAN.md §1.2.
src/fs/inode.mt
src/fs/perms.mt
src/fs/fdtable.mt
src/fs/tritfs.mt

# --- IPC --------------------------------------------------------------------
src/ipc/messages.mt
src/ipc/pipe.mt
src/ipc/trit_stream.mt

# --- drivers and syscalls ---------------------------------------------------
src/drivers/tty.mt
src/syscall/syscall.mt

# --- security ---------------------------------------------------------------
src/security/photon_cap.mt

# The kernel-side shell. In NEITHER manifest until 30 August 2026 (§4), so it
# was compiled by nothing; its `priv_name` had drifted from
# kernel/privilege.mt's because the flat namespace never made them meet.
src/userspace/shell.mt

# --- entry point (last: kernel_main calls everything above) -----------------
src/boot.mt
src/entry_kernel.mt
